// RemoteControlInterfaceMoveMessage Drive at a forward speed and turning rate. Send start-session first (it drives a docked device off its dock), and send this with waitForResult true: SUCCEEDED means the device has finished the motion, and the reply carries the resulting pose (same shape as spatial/pose), distanceMetres (straight-line displacement) and turnedDegrees (signed, positive is left; for a left turn it runs from -90 to 270 degrees and for a right turn from -270 to 90, so a 181 degree left turn reads +181, not -179), so no separate pose read is needed. A move is judged on what it asked for: a move with velocity must cover at least 5 cm, whatever it turned, and a turn in place (velocity 0) must turn at least 5 degrees. A move that is partly blocked still succeeds with the measured values; a move that did not do what it asked (a forward move that only turned or barely moved, or a turn that did not turn, because of an obstacle, a wall, its dock, or a camera request just before) fails with remote_control_move_not_carried_out, and the error message gives the measured distance, turn and pose, so change direction or resend. Duration must be a multiple of durationIncrement in the route's remote-control configuration, between minDuration and maxDuration; other values are rejected with value-out-of-range. Turn angle is omega x duration in seconds, so 90 degrees (pi/2 rad) is omega 0.785 for 2000 ms and 180 degrees (pi rad) is omega 0.785 for 4000 ms (positive turns left); distance is velocity x duration, so 0.2 m/s for 5000 ms covers about 1 m. The device drops remote control after about 30 s without a move, and the next move re-enters it first (a few seconds longer), so you do not need to send start-session again after a pause. Only a device back on its dock fails with remote_control_not_active: send start-session, which drives it off. Send one move at a time and wait for its result: a move (or start-session) that arrives while another is still running on the same device fails at once with remote_control_move_in_progress, and is never queued or allowed to preempt the running one (REST responseType SYNC or MCP waitForResult true; never fire-and-forget moves). Bounds are in the route's remote-control configuration.
type RemoteControlInterfaceMoveMessage struct {
	// Duration How long to hold this velocity and omega, in milliseconds. A multiple of durationIncrement between minDuration and maxDuration in the route's remote-control configuration.
	Duration int `json:"duration"`

	// Omega Turning rate in radians per second. Positive turns left (counter-clockwise seen from above), negative turns right.
	Omega float32 `json:"omega"`

	// SeqNum Sequence number for ordering moves. Starts at 1 after start-session and increases by one on each move; it keeps counting when the device has dropped remote control and the next move re-enters it.
	SeqNum int `json:"seqNum"`

	// Velocity Speed in metres per second. Positive drives forward, negative reverses.
	Velocity float32 `json:"velocity"`
}

