// RemoteControlInterfaceMoveResponseMessage The outcome of a move that was carried out, wholly or partly, measured from the device's pose before and after it. pose is where the device is now (the spatial/pose attribute, heading included), so no separate pose read is needed. distanceMetres is the straight-line displacement and turnedDegrees the signed heading change in the direction the move commanded (positive is left, counter-clockwise). A move that did not do what it asked is not a response; it fails with remote_control_move_not_carried_out.
type RemoteControlInterfaceMoveResponseMessage struct {
	// DistanceMetres Straight-line distance in metres between the pose before the move and the pose after it. It is displacement, not path length, so a move that drives out and back reads near zero.
	DistanceMetres float32 `json:"distanceMetres"`

	// Pose Live position of a located device (for example a robot vacuum) on a map. Unlike position, which labels a group, pose is where the device is now. point is a GeoJSON Point whose coordinates are [x, y] in metres in the frame named by relativeToGroupRouteId; heading and the other fields sit beside point, not inside it. The spatial/geometry attribute of the room groups (group_query over MCP, or GET /endpoint-groups with expand=interfaces.attributes over REST) carries the same relativeToGroupRouteId value, and the pose and the room shapes share one frame and unit, so they overlay with no conversion. containedInRoomGroupId names the room the device is in with the same group id group_query returns, so match it to a group directly. A docked device is at its charger, so its point is the charger position and it reports no heading. A device whose location is not known reports no pose.
	Pose SpatialInterfacePoseAttribute `json:"pose"`

	// TurnedDegrees Signed heading change in degrees, normalised in the direction the move commanded. A move with omega above 0 (a left turn) reports it in (-90, 270], a move with omega below 0 (a right turn) in [-270, 90), and a move with omega 0 in (-180, 180]. The largest turn a move can command is about 229 degrees, so a 4000 ms turn at omega 0.785 (about 181 degrees left) reads +181, not -179. Positive is a left (counter-clockwise) turn, negative a right turn.
	TurnedDegrees float32 `json:"turnedDegrees"`
}

